      description: 'Theory-level predictions preregistered on OSF: DOI 10.17605/OSF.IO/P8CKQ, registered 8 September 2026 (version 1.100), updated 13 September 2026 (version 1.102), updated 15 October 2026 (version 1.103), twenty-two propositions. Companion: Register · The ARC Theory · Operational Definitions, version 1.9.1, 5 October 2026.'
  abstract: |
    If the way a recursive process combines its gains fixes how its capability grows with depth, the growth of a self-improving system could be predicted from a property measured before it scales, while correction can still be built into it. This paper asks whether recursion amplifies capability in the same way across very different fields. Between 2024 and 2026, programmes in AI reasoning, quantum error correction, acoustic physics and consciousness science reported results that bear on recursive or recurrent processing, in different forms: a contested sequential advantage in AI reasoning, exponential suppression of error with code distance, a sustained limit cycle in a classical time crystal, and no measured form in neuroscience. We formalise the question with three axioms (separability, cumulative advantage, continuity) and a recursive composition operator. Under each case’s hypotheses, Cauchy’s functional equations give multiplicative composition a power law and additive composition an exponential; capped composition stays bounded. Inside the model the exponent is derived, not fitted: $\alpha = 1/(1-\beta)$ for coupling $\beta$; base potential and depth must interact multiplicatively; and a crossover depth $R^* = \alpha/a$, independent of base potential, separates approximately linear from compounding gains. Five structural properties follow, two on premises added to the axioms. The ARC Bound, $\alpha \leq 2$, is a conjecture: the value that Law III, the ARC Ceiling, a law under test, returns at $\gamma = 1/2$. The evidence is graded: the AI exponent’s best fit is one model’s and inconclusive, no exponent has been measured in a time crystal, and the neuroscience mapping is structural. Thirteen falsification criteria and a cross-domain forward prediction are stated; a computational suite checks the closed forms but tests no real system. Each result holds inside its stated model.

